From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] drm: a733: dp: .detect must report hot-plug, not "a DPCD read once worked"

`drm-a733-dp-register-a-drm-bridge` states its own contract in its vendor-comparison table:

  | .detect reads NO register and issues NO transaction. It returns what
  | .hpd_notify recorded. |

The code returns dp->dpcd_valid instead, and that is a different fact. Nothing
sets dpcd_valid but sun60i_dp_link_probe() (added by `drm-a733-dp-configure-the-main-link-phy`; sun60i-dp.c:1407-1418
-- every sun60i-dp.c line number here is the file as it stands AFTER `drm-a733-dp-instrument-the-training-failure-paths`, i.e.
the tree this patch applies to), and
every bring-up verb in this file calls it: `link` through
sun60i_dp_link_configure() (:1515) and `train` through sun60i_dp_train() (:2237).
So a connector that answers dpcd_valid answers "a user typed a command",
not "a display is plugged in".

MEASURED, the 8 GiB board, 2026-08-22: at boot
/sys/class/drm/card0-DP-1/status reads `disconnected`; after a single
`echo 0 > /sys/kernel/debug/5740000.displayport/train` it reads `connected`,
with no sink event of any kind and nothing physical touched. The connector is
lying, and it is lying in the direction that costs a measurement: `drm-a733-dp-register-a-drm-bridge` declines
DRM_BRIDGE_OP_HPD, so the connector is DRM_CONNECTOR_POLL_CONNECT |
POLL_DISCONNECT (drm_bridge_connector.c:845-848) and drm_probe_helper's
output_poll_execute() re-reads it every DRM_OUTPUT_POLL_PERIOD = 10*HZ
(drm_probe_helper.c:266, :807, :851). A status change from that poll reaches
drm_kms_helper_hotplug_event() (:848), which hands the link to the
drm_client_setup() client `drm-a733-kms-drm-device-for-the-display-pipe` registers -- an asynchronous modeset, i.e. a
second sun60i_dp_train() on a link somebody is measuring, and a TCON disable
on the way back down. On the run where this was checked explicitly it had NOT
fired (`main link configured` went 0 -> 3 for exactly three requested rungs,
and none of `drm-a733-dp-register-a-drm-bridge`'s atomic_enable-only error strings appeared), so this is a
live hazard and not an observed corruption. It is fixed before it is one.

THE SECOND DEFECT IN THE SAME FUNCTION PAIR, WHICH IS A LATCH
--------------------------------------------------------------

.detect does not end at its own return. drm_bridge_connector_detect() feeds the
value straight back into every bridge in the chain:

	status = detect->funcs->detect(detect, connector);
	if (hdmi)
		drm_atomic_helper_connector_hdmi_hotplug(connector, status);
	drm_bridge_connector_hpd_notify(connector, status);
					(drm_bridge_connector.c:214-219)

and sun60i_dp_bridge_hpd_notify() tears the channel down on anything that is
not connector_status_connected (sun60i-dp.c:2963-2967, calling
sun60i_dp_aux_disable(), which clears dpcd_valid at :1362 and enabled at :1363).

Compose the two and the driver has a one-way door. One failed
drm_dp_read_dpcd_caps() leaves dpcd_valid false; the next .detect -- at most ten
seconds later, and there is no way to stop it -- reports disconnected; the core
hands that back to .hpd_notify; .hpd_notify disables AUX. Every subsequent
detect reports disconnected because dpcd_valid can no longer become true with
the channel down, and nothing but a real out-of-band connected event reopens it.
On this board that event has never once arrived (the fwnode mismatch, `drm-a733-dp-register-a-drm-bridge`'s
OPEN 1), so on this board the door has no handle on the inside at all.

The same composition is why `echo 1 > aux_enable` is unstable today: it sets
enabled without setting dpcd_valid, so the next poll's detect->hpd_notify pair
calls sun60i_dp_aux_disable() on a channel the user just opened, within ten
seconds, silently. NOT separately measured -- it is read off the two call sites
above and labelled an inference for that reason. What makes it plausible rather
than merely derivable is that `echo 0 > train` survived on 2026-08-22: train
calls sun60i_dp_link_probe() first (:2237), so it sets dpcd_valid inside the
same dp->lock section and the poll finds `connected` afterwards. aux_enable on
its own sets no such thing.

WHAT THIS DOES
---------------

One new bool, hpd_asserted, which is what .hpd_notify recorded -- the thing
`drm-a733-dp-register-a-drm-bridge`'s table already promised .detect would return. Then:

1. .detect returns hpd_asserted. It still reads no register and issues no
   transaction, so `drm-a733-dp-register-a-drm-bridge`'s decision 1 -- DRM_BRIDGE_OP_DETECT is "affordable
   only because .detect touches nothing" -- and its WHY .detect TOUCHES NO
   REGISTER block both stand unchanged.

2. .hpd_notify becomes EDGE-triggered: it returns immediately when the status it
   is handed equals the state it already holds. This is not an optimisation, it
   is what makes 1. safe. .detect answers hpd_asserted, the core hands
   hpd_asserted straight back at drm_bridge_connector.c:219, and the poll
   therefore does nothing at all -- where a level-triggered .hpd_notify would
   turn every tenth second into either sun60i_dp_link_probe() (real AUX traffic
   from the poll, which is exactly the vendor behaviour `drm-a733-dp-register-a-drm-bridge`'s table calls out) or
   sun60i_dp_aux_disable().

3. .hpd_notify records the new state FIRST and unconditionally, before it
   touches the channel. That is what breaks the latch: hpd_asserted is a fact
   about the cable, not about this driver's success with it, so a failed
   sun60i_dp_aux_enable() or a failed sun60i_dp_link_probe() leaves the
   connector connected and the core free to retry, instead of reporting
   disconnected and being told to shut the channel it needs.

No event is lost by edge-triggering. drm_connector_oob_hotplug_event() from the
alternate mode carries only connected/disconnected
(drivers/usb/typec/altmodes/displayport.c:190-193, :214-215, :399-401); IRQ_HPD
is delivered by sysfs_notify() and never reaches DRM at all (:195-197). A
repeated "connected" from a Status Update is therefore a repeat, not a new
event, and dropping it is correct.

THE VENDOR DRIVER AND THIS DEFECT
---------------------------------

The vendor driver does not have this defect, so there is no comparison table.
This defect cannot exist in sunxi_drm_edp.c: it hand-rolls a connector with
DRM_CONNECTOR_POLL_HPD (edp.c:4033, quoted in `drm-a733-kms-drm-device-for-the-display-pipe`'s own table) and has no
drm_bridge at all, so there is no drm_bridge_connector_detect() to feed its
detect result back into a notify callback. The defect is ours, introduced by
`drm-a733-dp-register-a-drm-bridge`, and the vendor's connect path has the separate issue `drm-a733-dp-register-a-drm-bridge`
already records (a once-a-second DPCD poll into a powered-down front end).
What is genuinely A733 here is only which of the two flags a connector on THIS
board may answer, and that follows from the HPD_INPUT_STATE measurement `drm-a733-dp-register-a-drm-bridge`
already carries: the pad reads zero over a live link, so this driver can never
know hot-plug except by being told.

DIAGNOSTICS
------------

Nothing is deleted -- in this tree a working diagnostic is never removed, at most demoted. Two lines are added, and
both are cheap by construction:

  * the `link` debugfs file gains a `hot-plug :` row, so the state .detect
    answers is readable without dmesg and without provoking a poll. This is the
    file that already prints `sink caps : not read yet`, which is dpcd_valid --
    the two now sit next to each other and can be told apart by eye.
  * .hpd_notify logs one dev_info per TRANSITION. Edge-triggering is what makes
    that safe: it is one line per physical plug or unplug, never a per-poll
    line, and it is the observable that closes `drm-a733-dp-register-a-drm-bridge`'s OPEN 1 -- the first time
    this line appears on this board, the fwnode path works.

.detect itself still prints nothing, deliberately: it runs every ten seconds for
ever. Its silence is why this defect survived, so the answer is the debugfs row
above, not a print in the poll path.

⚠ NOT A FIX FOR THE MISSING HOT-PLUG, AND MUST NOT BE READ AS ONE. After this
patch the connector reports `disconnected` on this board and keeps reporting it,
because .hpd_notify still never fires -- drm_connector_oob_hotplug_event() from
typec_displayport targets the usb-c-connector node's fwnode while
drm_bridge_connector_init() gave our connector edp0's. That is `drm-a733-dp-register-a-drm-bridge`'s OPEN 1,
it is a separate defect with a separate fix (a drm_aux_hpd_bridge on the
connector node plus a next_bridge chain), and this patch deliberately touches
neither the fwnode, the DT, nor probe()/.attach so that the two do not collide.
What this patch changes is that the connector now says `disconnected` because it
has not been told otherwise, instead of saying `connected` because somebody ran
a debugfs command.

VERIFIED
---------

  applies clean : yes -- `patch -p1 --fuzz=0` on a freshly unpacked 6.18.36
                  carrying all 125 of patches/*.patch applied in order, i.e.
                  the full series up to and including
                  `drm-a733-dp-instrument-the-training-failure-paths`. Dry run and real apply both exit 0, no .rej, no .orig.
  builds        : yes -- `make ARCH=arm64 W=1 drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/` on that
                  tree with the series' own .config, native aarch64 gcc 14.2.0.
                  sun60i-dp.o and the other three modules compile with NO
                  warning. No new symbol is referenced: every identifier this
                  patch uses (dev_info, seq_printf, connector_status_connected,
                  guard(mutex)) is already used in this file.
  checkpatch    : `--strict --terse` reports 0 checks and 0 code warnings. The
                  two lines it does print -- the >75-char subject and "Missing
                  Signed-off-by by nominal patch author ''" -- are artefacts of
                  this tree's headerless patch format and are printed
                  identically for `drm-a733-dp-register-a-drm-bridge` and `drm-a733-dp-instrument-the-training-failure-paths`.
  on silicon    : NO. Not hardware-proven. The measurement that FOUND the bug is
                  hardware (see above); the fix is not.

FALSIFIER -- the exact measurement that found the defect, run again
--------------------------------------------------------------------

On the 8 GiB board, one boot, nothing physically touched at any point:

  cat /sys/class/drm/card0-DP-1/status              # expect: disconnected
  echo 1 > /sys/kernel/debug/5740000.displayport/aux_enable
  sleep 25                                          # >= 2 poll periods
  cat /sys/kernel/debug/5740000.displayport/link    # expect: aux up,
                                                    #   hot-plug never notified
  echo 0 > /sys/kernel/debug/5740000.displayport/train
  sleep 25
  cat /sys/class/drm/card0-DP-1/status              # expect: STILL disconnected
  cat /sys/kernel/debug/5740000.displayport/link    # expect: aux STILL up

REFUTED BY any of: `connected` after the train (the flag swap did not take);
`aux : down` after the sleep (the level trigger is still tearing the channel
down, i.e. the edge guard is not doing its job); a `hot-plug: sink present` line
in dmesg on a boot where nothing was plugged (hpd_asserted is being set from
somewhere other than a real notification); or any DPCD/AUX transaction visible
on the ten-second cadence.

CONFIRMED FURTHER, once the fwnode defect is fixed by the patch that owns it:
plugging the monitor produces exactly one `hot-plug: sink present`, the `link`
file's hot-plug row turns to `asserted`, and status turns `connected` -- and
unplugging produces exactly one `sink gone`. One line each, not one per poll.

OPEN
-----

1. A FAILED sun60i_dp_aux_enable() IS STILL NEVER RETRIED. The connector
   correctly stays `connected` -- the sink is there -- but nothing tries the
   channel again until the next real hot-plug edge. Observable: a boot where
   `hot-plug: sink present` is followed by no `AUX up` line and the `link` file
   reports `aux : down` for ever. Whether the retry belongs here or in the core
   is not settled and is not settled by guessing.

2. THE POLL IS NOW PURE, AND THAT MAKES IT USELESS ON THIS BOARD. With .detect
   reading nothing and .hpd_notify never firing, the ten-second poll can never
   change the answer. It is retained because it costs nothing and because it
   becomes correct the moment OPEN 1 of `drm-a733-dp-register-a-drm-bridge` is closed. If that fix does not
   land, the honest follow-up is to stop claiming DRM_BRIDGE_OP_DETECT at all
   rather than to poll a constant.

Assisted-by: Claude

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-dp.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-dp.c
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-dp.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-dp.c
@@ -329,6 +329,16 @@ struct sun60i_dp {
 	struct drm_dp_aux	aux;
 	struct mutex		lock;		/* serialises enable/disable */
 	bool			enabled;
+	/* What .hpd_notify last recorded, and the ONLY thing .detect reports.
+	 * This bridge does not claim DRM_BRIDGE_OP_HPD and cannot read
+	 * hot-plug itself, so nothing but an out-of-band notification can
+	 * ever set it: false is the correct state at bind and probe sets
+	 * nothing (devm_drm_bridge_alloc() zeroes the struct).
+	 *
+	 * Deliberately NOT dpcd_valid, which says the sink answered a DPCD
+	 * read -- a different question, asked by every debugfs verb.
+	 */
+	bool			hpd_asserted;
 	/* The sink's own capability bytes, and what was last asked of the PHY.
 	 * rate is in Mb/s, the unit phy_configure_opts_dp counts in.
 	 */
@@ -2286,6 +2296,25 @@ static int sun60i_dp_train(struct sun60i_dp *dp, unsigned int rate_mbps,
  * into a powered-down front end, forever instead of only at bind. So .detect
  * reports state that .hpd_notify recorded, and only .hpd_notify opens the
  * channel.
+ *
+ * AND WHY .hpd_notify IS EDGE-TRIGGERED, which is the other half of the same
+ * sentence and was missing until 2026-08-22. .detect does not end at its own
+ * return: drm_bridge_connector_detect() feeds the value straight back into
+ * every bridge in the chain --
+ *
+ *	status = detect->funcs->detect(detect, connector);
+ *	...
+ *	drm_bridge_connector_hpd_notify(connector, status);
+ *					(drm_bridge_connector.c:214-219)
+ *
+ * -- so a .hpd_notify that ACTS on the status it is handed turns the ten-second
+ * poll into a ten-second AUX transaction (on connected) or a ten-second channel
+ * teardown (on disconnected), and .detect's promise to touch nothing becomes
+ * false by a route that is invisible in this file. Acting only on a CHANGE of
+ * the recorded state closes that loop: .detect answers hpd_asserted,
+ * .hpd_notify is handed hpd_asserted back, and nothing happens. The alternate
+ * mode delivers only connected/disconnected through this path -- IRQ_HPD goes
+ * to sysfs and never to DRM (displayport.c:190-197) -- so no event is lost.
  */
 
 /*
@@ -2396,13 +2425,21 @@ sun60i_dp_bridge_detect(struct drm_bridge *bridge,
 	guard(mutex)(&dp->lock);
 
 	/*
-	 * dpcd_valid, not `enabled`: the AUX channel being powered says this
-	 * driver opened it, the sink's own capability bytes say something
-	 * answered. No sink is a STATE, not an error -- so this
-	 * returns disconnected quietly and logs nothing on the ten-second poll.
+	 * The hot-plug state, and nothing else. NOT dpcd_valid: that flag says
+	 * a DPCD read succeeded, which every debugfs verb makes true --
+	 * sun60i_dp_link_probe() is called by `link` and by `train` -- so a
+	 * connector answering it reports "connected" because a user typed
+	 * a command, with no sink event of any kind. Measured on the 8 GiB board on
+	 * 2026-08-22: status reads disconnected at
+	 * boot and connected after one `echo 0 > .../train`, with nothing
+	 * physical touched.
+	 *
+	 * No sink is a STATE, not an error, so this returns
+	 * disconnected quietly and logs nothing on the ten-second poll. What it
+	 * reports is visible without dmesg in the `link` file's hot-plug line.
 	 */
-	return dp->dpcd_valid ? connector_status_connected
-			      : connector_status_disconnected;
+	return dp->hpd_asserted ? connector_status_connected
+				: connector_status_disconnected;
 }
 
 static const struct drm_edid *
@@ -2445,11 +2482,34 @@ static void sun60i_dp_bridge_hpd_notify(struct drm_bridge *bridge,
 					enum drm_connector_status status)
 {
 	struct sun60i_dp *dp = bridge_to_dp(bridge);
+	bool asserted = status == connector_status_connected;
 	int ret;
 
 	guard(mutex)(&dp->lock);
 
-	if (status != connector_status_connected) {
+	/*
+	 * EDGE, not level, and this guard is load-bearing rather than an
+	 * optimisation. drm_bridge_connector_detect() calls this function with
+	 * the result of our own .detect (drm_bridge_connector.c:214-219), so on
+	 * a level trigger every ten-second poll would re-open, re-probe or tear
+	 * down the channel -- see the block above sun60i_dp_bridge_link_budget().
+	 */
+	if (asserted == dp->hpd_asserted)
+		return;
+
+	/*
+	 * Recorded FIRST, and unconditionally, because it is a fact about the
+	 * cable and not about this driver's success with it. A version that set
+	 * it only once the channel came up would latch the connector off after
+	 * one failed AUX transaction: .detect would report disconnected, the
+	 * core would hand that back here, and this function would disable a
+	 * channel nothing could then re-enable short of a physical re-plug.
+	 */
+	dp->hpd_asserted = asserted;
+	dev_info(dp->dev, "hot-plug: sink %s\n",
+		 asserted ? "present" : "gone");
+
+	if (!asserted) {
 		if (dp->enabled)
 			sun60i_dp_aux_disable(dp);
 		return;
@@ -2462,8 +2522,9 @@ static void sun60i_dp_bridge_hpd_notify(struct drm_bridge *bridge,
 	}
 
 	/* Failure is reported by sun60i_dp_link_probe() itself and leaves
-	 * dpcd_valid false, which is what .detect will then answer. Nothing is
-	 * retried here -- the DRM core owns AUX retries.
+	 * dpcd_valid false -- which .detect no longer answers, so a sink that
+	 * is physically there stays connected and the core is free to retry the
+	 * EDID. Nothing is retried here; the DRM core owns AUX retries.
 	 */
 	sun60i_dp_link_probe(dp);
 }
